  <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;Structuring AI assistant guidance as &lt;strong&gt;modular skill files&lt;/strong&gt; rather than one monolithic document, and what that buys you on a repository too large to hold in context.&lt;/p&gt;</summary>

  <content type="xhtml">
    <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>A single instructions file is <strong>read in full, every time</strong>. Past a certain repository size that is neither affordable nor useful, and the file drifts out of date because nothing exercises it.</p>

      <h2>What works better</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Split guidance by task, not by subsystem</li>
        <li>State what the build gate actually checks, with the command</li>
        <li>Record the reasoning behind a rule, so it can be revisited rather than cargo-culted</li>
        <li>Cite file and line, so a claim can be checked</li>
        <li>Say what is deliberately out of scope</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Conclusion</h2>
      <p>Guidance for an assistant is documentation with a consumer, and it rots exactly the way documentation rots.</p>
    </div>
  </content>
